    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Ubaid culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euphrates.jpg" alt="Euphrates" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Euphrates <span class="kid-tale-cr">Carole Raddato from Frankfurt, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 8,500 years ago, people began to settle on the flat river lands of southern Iraq.</p><p>Archaeologists call their way of life the Ubaid culture, after a place called Tell al-Ubaid.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ubaid-period.jpg" alt="Ubaid period" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Ubaid period <span class="kid-tale-cr">CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They lived in big villages of mud-brick houses and built the first temples in Mesopotamia.</p><p>Their pots were painted with black patterns on a pale yellow-brown background.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flax.jpg" alt="Flax" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Flax <span class="kid-tale-cr">Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Farmers grew barley, wheat, lentils and flax, and kept sheep, goats, cattle and pigs.</p><p>On the coast, people fished and even caught tuna.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>A clay disc from Kuwait shows a boat with two masts, the oldest sign of sails anywhere.</p><p>Their pots spread along the Persian Gulf, perhaps traded for dates, shell jewellery or fish.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arslantepe.jpg" alt="Arslantepe" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Arslantepe <span class="kid-tale-cr">Zeynel Cebeci, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their ideas and styles spread north as far as Turkey, mostly in peace rather than by war.</p><p>They buried their dead with beads, necklaces and pots, perhaps holding food.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uruk.jpg" alt="Uruk" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Uruk <span class="kid-tale-cr">Picture taken by Marcus Cyron, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 5,800 years ago, the Ubaid age gave way to the Uruk period, when the first real cities grew.</p><p>Their temples, painted pots and graves, dug up by archaeologists, still tell their story.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Ubaid period left the earliest known settlements on the southern Mesopotamian plain, in villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> houses with the first temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</li><li>Ubaid pottery and customs spread from south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, according to Gil Stein and Rana Özbal largely through the peaceful spread of ideas rather than conquest.</li><li>Graves show growing differences in wealth and rank, but there were still no real cities: those came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a> that followed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A clay disc from the site of H3 in Kuwait shows a two-masted boat, the oldest evidence for the use of masts and sails.</p></aside>
    </section>

